WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:04.640
What's up guys my name is Colin McNeil for theScore esports.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:08.560
You know there's nothing quite like a grand final.

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:16.480
The crowd is intense, there's everything to play for, and victory is so close you can just about taste it.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:20.360
Whether it's the final match in an incredible lower bracket run.

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:23.400
Or another shot at the coveted Aegis.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:29.080
Grand finals have given us some of the most memorable Dota 2 matches of all time.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:36.780
So sit back, hit that subscribe button, and get ready as we count down the Top 10 Most Memorable Dota 2

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:37.680
Grand Finals.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.580
Coming in at number 10 is Team Secret vs. Team Liquid at The Shanghai Major.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:49.000
The Shanghai Major was known for many things.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:53.980
Poor production, participants playing without proper equipment, and of course,

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:57.620
the public firing of James “2GD” Harding.

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:02.040
But that shouldn’t detract from the exciting Dota that was played.

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:05.400
Team Secret may have knocked Team Liquid out of the upper bracket

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:11.240
But Liquid defeated reigning TI winners Evil Geniuses in the loser bracket finals

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:15.180
to give them another shot at Team Secret in the grand finals.

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:19.860
"I'm actually really surprised, I think going in I would have picked neither team to even make top four,

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:21.440
maybe top six."

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:26.880
(Casting)

00:01:26.880 --> 00:02:08.200
(Casting)

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:15.200
Team Secret defeated Liquid 3-1, marking the the first time that this super team won a Valve event.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:20.040
"What a game that was, I think it is only fitting to see Team Secret end

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:25.680
with such a dominant game as they did, truly your Shanghai Major Champions."

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:32.200
Coming in at number 9 is Alliance vs. iNfernity at RaidCall EMS Summer Cup #4

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:37.840
In the summer of 2013, Alliance were one of the most dominant teams in Dota 2.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:51.740
(Casting)

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:57.920
Which is why it was no surprise that the Swedish team faced little resistance on their path

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:00.700
to this smaller tournament’s grand final.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:05.520
But that’s where things got interesting, as caster TobiWan issued them a challenge:

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:11.220
"The challenge which was sent to Alliance by myself as jokingish and then they actually said okay

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:14.100
was actually to get a racks in ten minutes."

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:18.940
While the challenge was issued as a joke, support player Akke accepted it

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:21.480
and the team drafted a heavy push comp.

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:31.600
(Casting)

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:37.480
Alliance not only managed to take a lane of racks, but beat iNfernity is eight minutes

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:39.950
and forty-three seconds.

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:44.860
iNfernity would get their revenge in Game 2, but Alliance ultimately won the series.

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:54.780
Coming in at number eight is Mineski beating LGD Gaming at the Dota 2 Asia Championship 2018.

00:03:54.780 --> 00:04:01.720
While Southeast Asia was always seen as a strong region, this tournament saw them win their first Dota 2

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:02.360
Major.

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:05.580
And they did it, by taking on some of Dota’s best teams.

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:23.660
(Casting)

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:29.380
The grand final saw Mineski go face-to-face with one of China’s best, LGD Gaming.

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:34.640
The two teams went back-and forth in a series that went all five games.

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:38.200
In the end Mineski were the ones left standing.

00:04:38.200 --> 00:05:12.840
(Casting)

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:18.660
Coming in at number seven is OG vs. Team Secret at The Frankfurt Major.

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:21.160
Despite a poor showing in the group stage,

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:26.640
OG put together a historic run that saw them take the lower bracket by storm.

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:31.340
"Blitz, it has been a miraculous run, some might say for OG."

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:38.100
"They have taken down Fnatic, Mineski, Virtus.Pro, CDEC, and EHOME to get here they are 9-2

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:45.340
in their last 11 games. The green dream is strong but the boys in blue, they stand in opposition."

00:05:45.440 --> 00:06:18.300
(Casting)

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:21.300
In the first ever Dota 2 Major Grand Final,

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:26.200
OG would come face-to-face with a team that they had some personal beef with:

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:27.760
Team Secret.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:33.700
Earlier in the year both Fly and N0tail left Secret, a team that they had helped found.

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:41.440
Team Secret was the final obstacle standing in the way of the greatest lower bracket run in Dota 2 history.

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:58.800
(Casting)

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:02.660
OG would go on to beat Team Secret 3-1 in the series,

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:09.420
defeating their rivals and putting together a tournament run that will be remembered for years to come.

00:07:09.820 --> 00:07:17.680
"A huge run, the dream run, achieving what no other team has done and no other team will probably achieve

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:19.260
for a very long time."

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:27.040
Coming in at number six is Alliance vs. LGD at the G-1 Champions League Season Five.

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:32.960
At the time, China was widely regarded as the most dominant region in Dota 2,

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:39.020
But Alliance were making waves in the EU scene, and many Dota 2 fans wanted to see them take

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:41.400
on the world’s best teams.

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:47.220
"This is even better in my opinion, because you have Alliance. The foreigner, the weaker teams coming from

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:49.920
this unknown country to these Chinese guys

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:53.140
to China and they are just beating up on everybody."

00:07:53.420 --> 00:07:59.180
As the main tournament began, Alliance and Liquid were tapped as the west’s only representatives.

00:07:59.180 --> 00:08:04.540
TL put up a poor showing in the group stage, but Alliance more than made up for it,

00:08:04.540 --> 00:08:10.660
going 5-0 in their group stage, and landing an automatic spot in the Grand Finals.

00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:17.050
There, the future TI winners were set to go head-to-head with one of Asia’s most powerful

00:08:17.050 --> 00:08:20.160
Dota 2 teams, LGD Gaming.

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:22.240
But that didn't phase Alliance.

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:51.300
(Casting)

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:57.900
Coming in at number five is OG vs. Virtus.pro at The Kiev Major.

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:02.420
The best-of-five final was a back-and-forth affair, but the fifth game was the one that

00:09:02.420 --> 00:09:07.240
showed the world why OG could arguably be considered a dynasty.

00:09:07.240 --> 00:09:10.300
"Let's get our final predictions for game five."

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:11.580
"Who do we curse?"

00:09:11.660 --> 00:09:15.420
"Can you just be careful that's all I'm going to say."

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:16.800
"Ahh Godz."

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:22.900
"Game five of the Major, I'm gonna go with what my gut says, and that's that OG they're the three time Major

00:09:22.900 --> 00:09:24.200
Champions for a reason."

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:27.680
An aggressive draft from VP caught OG by surprise.

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:30.300
"They pick an Enchantress, that is..."

00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:31.320
"Wow"

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:32.200
"That's ballsy"

00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:34.860
"Oh and they take the Alchemist themselves."

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:39.180
And the European team played most of the first half on their heels.

00:09:39.240 --> 00:10:07.120
(Casting)

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:11.200
With their backs against
the wall, OG brought the fight to VP.

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:56.480
(Casting)

00:10:57.520 --> 00:11:02.360
At number four is OG versus Ad Finem at The Boston Major.

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:08.800
During qualifiers, Ad Finem managed to sweep both Team Secret and Team Liquid, defying pretty

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:13.760
much everybody’s expectations. After making it through the group stage, it looked like

00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:16.080
Ad Finem couldn’t be stopped.

00:11:16.280 --> 00:11:17.620
First, Newbee.

00:11:17.620 --> 00:11:26.160
(Casting)

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:28.240
Second LFY.

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:38.240
(Casting)

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:40.440
Then, Digital Chaos.

00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:55.840
(Casting)

00:11:55.840 --> 00:12:00.440
And then, the final boss, OG. But while OG were favorites

00:12:00.440 --> 00:12:07.120
heading into the tournament, the greek squad had, heart, soul, and a chant that took over the venue.

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:18.600
While OG had their number most of the game, it was Maybe Next Time’s courageous play

00:12:18.600 --> 00:12:21.440
that took the hearts of Dota fans everywhere.

00:12:21.560 --> 00:12:42.400
(Casting)

00:12:42.680 --> 00:12:45.600
The team may not have won the series or the Major,

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:49.720
but Ad Finem stood tall in the face of a Dota 2 giant, managed to draw blood,

00:12:49.720 --> 00:12:55.440
They managed to draw blood, and capture the hearts of Dota 2 fans everywhere.

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:56.680
"It's like it was scripted."

00:12:57.480 --> 00:12:59.000
"It's like it was Ben."

00:12:59.360 --> 00:13:04.960
"I've never seen you lose it like that. What about you guys Alex? Did you guys lose it as hard as we did?"

00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:14.600
"Seriously, I am blown a way. What an absolute f***ing treat."

00:13:15.640 --> 00:13:22.840
Taking the number three spot we got Newbee vs. Team Liquid at TI7. Imagine, playing on all the right

00:13:22.840 --> 00:13:25.360
teams, but at the wrong time.

00:13:25.360 --> 00:13:27.480
This was the reality for Kuroky

00:13:27.920 --> 00:13:34.220
He would always play on top tier teams, but could never capture the ever-elusive Aegis.

00:13:34.220 --> 00:13:41.120
At TI7 Team Liquid had to fight their way through the dreaded lower bracket to make it to the finals.

00:13:41.120 --> 00:14:32.280
(Casting)

00:14:32.280 --> 00:14:37.800
It took Kuroky four years before he returned to an International grand final,

00:14:37.900 --> 00:14:43.220
and all that was standing in his way was one of China’s top teams: Newbee.

00:14:43.220 --> 00:14:46.860
This time he would not let the Aegis slip away.

00:14:47.100 --> 00:14:48.360
"Don't forget our motto."

00:14:48.600 --> 00:14:51.140
"Don't give a f***."

00:14:51.140 --> 00:14:56.040
"The less f***s we give, the better we will play. I waited four f***ing years for this day."

00:14:56.680 --> 00:14:58.600
"This time we're gonna rewrite history."

00:14:58.600 --> 00:15:01.440
"This could be the final game of TI."

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:07.480
"Newbee are down 0-2. Team Liquid has made an extraordinary run winning six consecutive series

00:15:07.520 --> 00:15:08.480
to get here."

00:15:08.480 --> 00:15:49.140
(Casting)

00:15:49.140 --> 00:15:52.800
Kuroky would finally become a TI Champion.

00:15:52.800 --> 00:16:01.280
"And they're about to do exactly that, your champions of The International 2017 is Team Liquid!"

00:16:03.120 --> 00:16:09.400
Taking the runner-up spot on this list is CDEC versus EG at TI five,

00:16:09.400 --> 00:16:14.820
a series that will forever be remembered fo one single play.

00:16:15.040 --> 00:16:20.100
A play that could only happen if all the pieces are in the right place.

00:16:20.100 --> 00:16:25.880
A play that will go down in history as THE
six-million dollar echo slam.

00:16:26.080 --> 00:16:44.500
(Casting)

00:16:44.560 --> 00:16:48.560
It was one of the most incredible plays in Dota 2 history

00:16:48.560 --> 00:16:51.620
and while the series had its fair share of memorable moments,

00:16:51.840 --> 00:16:53.760
this one took the cake.

00:16:53.760 --> 00:16:57.940
"We were in control of the game but they had just picked off SumaiL and they were trying to take Rosh."

00:16:58.020 --> 00:17:03.400
"And they all walked into the pit. We didn't have vision of them at the time but then I told pp to throw

00:17:03.400 --> 00:17:07.920
in an Ice Vortex. And he threw it in there and I saw a couple of them so I just assumed they'd all be in there."

00:17:07.920 --> 00:17:12.300
"And I just made a blind leap of faith. And they were all in there and they all died."

00:17:12.740 --> 00:17:18.060
"My first reaction after the Echo Slam I just yelled. I don't even know what's coming out of my mouth"

00:17:18.320 --> 00:17:19.600
"It was pretty loud."

00:17:19.700 --> 00:17:23.300
It was the play that made EG, TI Champions.

00:17:23.480 --> 00:17:27.040
A play that made EG, six million dollars richer.

00:17:28.480 --> 00:17:34.860
Finishing off our list at number one is, Na`vi versus Alliance at TI three.

00:17:35.060 --> 00:17:39.280
As the only TI final that needed all five game to crown a victor,

00:17:39.280 --> 00:17:44.800
the nearly three-million dollar prize pool was essentially left to a best of one.

00:17:44.800 --> 00:17:50.580
"It all comes down to this, one more game, two million dollars on the table,

00:17:50.580 --> 00:17:57.660
winner, first place at The International, The Aegis, and the prestige of being the best Dota 2 team in the world."

00:17:57.700 --> 00:18:01.440
When the final game began, both teams were considered equal,

00:18:01.440 --> 00:18:05.440
and victory for either was as certain as a coin toss.

00:18:05.440 --> 00:18:09.920
Alliance trailed most of the game, and Na`Vi appeared to have their number.

00:18:09.940 --> 00:18:14.200
Then… S4 did something that amazed everyone.

00:18:14.200 --> 00:18:36.440
(Casting)

00:18:36.440 --> 00:18:40.400
Dream Coil, after Dream Coil, after Dream Coil.

00:18:40.400 --> 00:18:44.520
S4 managed to completely halt Na`Vi’s momentum.

00:18:44.520 --> 00:19:10.040
(Casting)

00:19:10.120 --> 00:19:15.320
This would go down in the minds of Dota 2
fans everywhere, as one of the greatest games

00:19:15.320 --> 00:19:16.800
EVER played.

00:19:16.800 --> 00:19:20.460
"They are your International 3 Champions."

00:19:20.460 --> 00:19:25.000
"They have taken home the first ever International for the Swedes."

00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:31.620
Alright guys that's our list. Now if your favourite Dota 2 grand final was not on it, consider writing an angry

00:19:31.620 --> 00:19:35.140
incoherent five hundred word rant in the comment section below.

00:19:36.060 --> 00:19:40.680
Thanks for watching. If you want more great content just like this, be sure to hit the subscribe button.

